  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 320 AM EST Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Frost Advisory in effect tonight for south-\ncentral VA and interior portions of southeast VA and northeast NC. \nDry and seasonably mild otherwise through mid-week. \n\nA dry cold front passed through the area earlier today and high \npressure is building back in behind it. Latest obs show wind \ngusts 20-25mph west of the bay and 30mph+ over the Eastern \nShore. Winds are forecast to quickly diminish after sunset \nbecoming calm or near calm by 7pm. From there, temperatures \nshould quickly drop off as clear skies and little to no wind \nprovide ideal conditions for radiational cooling. Keeping this \nin mind, the forecast overnight lows undercut most of the \nguidance, since most models do not capture decoupling well \nenough. The FA is looking at lows in the mid 30s for most of the\narea and around 40 immediately near the coast. Would not be \nsurprised if a few of the more isolated, rural locations were to\ndrop to freezing. Frost is likely for interior portions of the \narea. The growing season has already ended for the western \npiedmont counties, so no advisory is needed there. Frost \nAdvisories have been issued, though, for interior portions of \nsoutheast VA and northeast NC. Conditions on Tuesday and \nWednesday look fairly nice with mild temperatures for mid- \nNovember. Highs will be around 70 each day.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A strong cold front brings chances for rain \nThursday-Friday, but chances for appreciable rain is low. Much \ncooler temperatures follow behind the front. \n\nGuidance continues to signal an anomalously strong 500mb trough \ndigging in over the east coast Thursday into the weekend. While\nthere is some uncertainty in the positions of the upper level \nlow and the positioning/track of associated sfc low pressure(s),\nthere is confidence in the UL trough itself dipping as far \nsouth as the Gulf. Regarding sfc systems, the 12z suite of \nglobal models are leaning toward low pressure diving ESE out of \nthe lower Great Lakes before developing a secondary low \noffshore. That kind of set up is likely to put the local area in\nthe dry slot. This is corroborated by the QPF probs from the \nensembles, which universally limit appreciable rainfall to our \nnorthern counties. Probs for QPF >0.1\" are 50-60% along and \nnorth of I-64 at best. Probs for >0.5\" are under 20% everywhere.\nWhat precip we do get should move in by Thursday afternoon and \npotentially continue through Friday along the coast. Highs \nFriday-Sunday will be a lot cooler with highs in the upper 40s \nand low 50s. Gusty conditions will keep apparent temperatures\neven lower. Overnight lows near or below freezing will be \npossible each night.",
